// Unit 2 "Stocks", Chapter 5: Strategy, Risk & Your Brain.
export default {
  id: 'stocks-ch5',
  title: 'Strategy, Risk & Your Brain',
  blurb: 'The part that decides whether you last: risk math, position sizing, trading styles, psychology, testing and building a real portfolio.',
  lessons: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
    {
      id: 'stocks-5-risk-first',
      title: 'Risk First: The Math of Staying Alive',
      summary: 'Why pros obsess over losses before gains: the 1-2% rule, the brutal math of recovering from drawdowns, and risk of ruin.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'shield',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Defense wins championships', html: `<p>Beginners ask, "How much can I make?" Experienced traders ask, "How much can I lose, and can I survive it?" That flip in thinking is the whole difference.</p><p>Here is why. In trading, you will be wrong a lot. Even solid strategies routinely lose on 40% to 60% of trades. If one bad trade can wipe out a big chunk of your account, it is only a matter of time before a bad streak does exactly that.</p><p>So the first job is not finding winners. It is <span class="hl">making sure no single loss, and no ordinary losing streak, can knock you out of the game</span>. A trader who survives gets to keep learning. A trader who blows up gets a very expensive lesson and an empty account.</p><p>This chapter is about that defense: how much to risk, how to size trades, where to put stops, and how your own brain tries to sabotage all of it.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Rule one: don't lose the account. Rule two: see rule one. Everything else is details." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The 1-2% rule', html: `<p>The most common guideline among professional and serious retail traders: <span class="hl">risk no more than 1% to 2% of your account on any single trade</span>.</p><p>Read that carefully. It does <em>not</em> mean "put only 1% of your money into a trade." It means the amount you would <strong>lose if your stop-loss gets hit</strong> should be 1% to 2% of the account.</p><p>Example: Leo has a $10,000 practice account. At 1% risk, his maximum loss per trade is <strong>$100</strong>. He could buy $3,000 worth of a stock, as long as his stop is placed so that getting stopped out costs about $100. (Next lesson shows exactly how to calculate that.)</p><p>Many beginners start at 0.5% or 1% while they are still learning, since their mistakes are bigger and more frequent. The point is not the exact number. The point is choosing it <em>in advance</em>, sticking to it, and keeping it small enough that losing ten in a row hurts but does not end you.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Maya has a $2,500 account and risks 2% per trade. What is the most she should lose on one trade?', answer: 50, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '$2,500 x 0.02 = $50. That is the loss if her stop is hit, not the size of the position.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Survival math: losses hurt more than gains help', html: `<p>Here is the math that scares every experienced trader straight. When you lose a percentage, you need a <strong>bigger</strong> percentage gain to get back to even, because you are now growing a smaller pile.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Loss</th><th>Gain needed to recover</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class="down">-10%</span></td><td><span class="up">+11.1%</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="down">-20%</span></td><td><span class="up">+25%</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="down">-33%</span></td><td><span class="up">+50%</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="down">-50%</span></td><td><span class="up">+100%</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="down">-75%</span></td><td><span class="up">+300%</span></td></tr><tr><td><span class="down">-90%</span></td><td><span class="up">+900%</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Lose half of $10,000 and you have $5,000. To get back to $10,000, that $5,000 has to <em>double</em>. The formula is: <strong>gain needed = loss / (1 - loss)</strong>. For a 50% loss: 0.5 / 0.5 = 1, which is 100%.</p><p>Small losses are easy to climb out of. Big ones turn into a hole that can take years, or forever.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'wow', say: "Down 50% needs up 100%. Percentages are not symmetric. This one fact has saved more accounts than any indicator." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'An account falls 40%. What percentage gain is needed to get back to where it started? (Round to one decimal.)', answer: 66.7, tolerance: 0.2, unit: '%', explain: '0.40 / (1 - 0.40) = 0.40 / 0.60 = 0.667, about 66.7%. A $10,000 account down to $6,000 needs $4,000 more, which is 66.7% of $6,000.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Losing streaks are normal', html: `<p>Say a strategy wins half its trades. How often will it lose several in a row? More often than your gut expects. Over a few hundred trades, runs of six or seven straight losses are completely normal for a coin-flip-style win rate. It does not mean the strategy is broken. It is just randomness.</p><p>Now compare two traders who each hit a streak of 10 losses:</p><ul><li><strong>Risking 1% per trade:</strong> each loss takes 1% of what is left. After 10 losses, the account is at about 0.99 to the 10th power, roughly <strong>90%</strong> of where it started. Down about 10%. Annoying. Recoverable with an 11% gain.</li><li><strong>Risking 10% per trade:</strong> after 10 losses, the account is at 0.90 to the 10th power, roughly <strong>35%</strong> of where it started. Down about 65%. Now it needs nearly a 190% gain to recover.</li></ul><p>Same strategy, same bad luck. One trader shrugs. The other is basically finished. <span class="hl">Position size, not strategy, decided who survived.</span></p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If a strategy has a 50% win rate, losing 6 trades in a row means the strategy has stopped working.', answer: false, explain: 'Streaks like that are expected over a few hundred trades even with a 50% win rate. You judge a strategy over a large sample, not one streak, which is why small risk per trade matters.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Risk of ruin', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="The probability that a trader loses so much that they cannot keep trading, or hit a loss level they decided they would not go past.">Risk of ruin</span> is the chance that you lose enough money to be knocked out of the game: either the account is wiped out, or it drops below the point where you would quit.</p><p>It depends on three things:</p><ol><li><strong>Win rate:</strong> how often you are right.</li><li><strong>Payoff:</strong> how big your wins are compared with your losses.</li><li><strong>Bet size:</strong> how much of your account you risk each time.</li></ol><p>The first two are hard to control. Markets decide. The third is <em>entirely</em> up to you. Even a strategy with a genuine edge has a meaningful risk of ruin if you bet too big, because a normal bad streak arrives before the edge has time to play out. And a strategy with no edge will eventually grind you down no matter how small you bet. Small sizing buys time; it does not create an edge.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "You cannot control whether the next trade wins. You can always control how much it costs if it doesn't." } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Dev learns the hard way', html: `<p>Dev turned $5,000 into $8,000 in two weeks by putting his whole account into each trade. He felt like a genius. Then three trades in a row went against him by 10%, 12% and 15%. His account fell to about $5,400. Desperate to "make it back fast," he went all in on a hyped stock that dropped 28% the next morning. He was below $4,000.</p><p>Over that stretch he had more winning trades than losing ones. Sizing, not stock picking, sank him. At 1% risk per trade, the same four losses would have cost him about 4% of his account, and he would have had a calm head for the next trade instead of a panicked one.</p>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Order these losses from easiest to hardest to recover from.', items: ['A 10% loss', 'A 25% loss', 'A 50% loss', 'A 80% loss'], explain: 'Required gains: 11.1%, 33.3%, 100%, and 400%. The deeper the hole, the faster the required gain explodes.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Risk words', cards: [
          { front: 'Risk per trade', back: 'The dollars you lose if your stop is hit. Not the size of the position.' },
          { front: '1-2% rule', back: 'Keep the loss on any single trade to 1-2% of the account.' },
          { front: 'Drawdown', back: 'The drop from an account peak to a later low, shown as a percent.' },
          { front: 'Losing streak', back: 'Several losses in a row. Normal, even for good strategies.' },
          { front: 'Risk of ruin', back: 'The chance of losing so much you cannot keep going.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Under the 1% rule, what does the 1% refer to?', options: ['The share of the account put into the trade', 'The amount lost if the stop-loss is hit', 'The expected profit', 'The commission'], answer: 1, explain: 'The 1% is the planned loss if you are wrong, not the position size.' },
          { q: 'After a 50% loss, what gain is needed to break even?', options: ['50%', '75%', '200%', '100%'], answer: 3, explain: '$10,000 becomes $5,000. Getting back to $10,000 means doubling: +100%.' },
          { q: 'Leo risks 1% per trade and hits 10 losses in a row. Roughly how much is his account down?', options: ['About 10%', 'About 1%', 'About 50%', 'About 100%'], answer: 0, explain: '0.99 to the 10th power is about 0.904, so roughly a 10% drawdown. Painful, survivable.' },
          { q: 'Which factor affecting risk of ruin is fully under the trader\'s control?', options: ['Win rate', 'Market volatility', 'How much is risked per trade', 'News events'], answer: 2, explain: 'You choose your bet size. Win rate and volatility depend on the market.' },
          { q: 'Dev wins 6 of his first 10 trades but risks his whole account each time. What is the biggest problem?', options: ['His win rate is too low', 'He should only trade on Mondays', 'Winning 60% is suspicious', 'One normal losing streak can wipe him out regardless of his win rate'], answer: 3, explain: 'Oversized bets mean an ordinary streak causes losses too deep to recover from.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
    {
      id: 'stocks-5-position-sizing',
      title: 'Position Sizing: How Many Shares?',
      summary: 'One formula turns your risk rule into an exact share count. Learn it, use it on every trade, and know where it can break.',
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'calculator',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The question nobody asks first', html: `<p>Most beginners decide how many shares to buy by vibes. "I'll grab 100 shares." "I'll put in $1,000." "I'll go big, this one's a sure thing."</p><p>Pros work backward. They decide <strong>how much they are willing to lose</strong> first, then <strong>where the trade is proven wrong</strong> (the stop), and only then calculate the number of shares. The share count is the <em>output</em>, not the input.</p><p>This is called <span class="term" data-def="Choosing how many shares to buy so that a loss at your stop equals a fixed, planned amount of your account.">position sizing</span>, and it is the single most practical skill in this chapter. It connects your risk rule from last lesson to an actual order you can place.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'happy', say: "I never pick a share count by feel. I compute it. Every trade. You can too, it is one division." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The formula', html: `<p>Here it is:</p><p><span class="hl">Shares = (Account x Risk %) / (Entry - Stop)</span></p><ul><li><strong>Account x Risk %</strong> is your dollar risk: the most you are willing to lose on this trade.</li><li><strong>Entry - Stop</strong> is your risk per share: how much each share loses if the stop is hit.</li></ul><p>Divide one by the other and you get the number of shares where hitting your stop costs exactly your planned amount.</p><p><strong>Example.</strong> Leo has a $10,000 account and risks 1%. He wants to buy at $50 with a stop at $48.</p><ol><li>Dollar risk: $10,000 x 0.01 = <strong>$100</strong></li><li>Risk per share: $50 - $48 = <strong>$2</strong></li><li>Shares: $100 / $2 = <strong>50 shares</strong></li></ol><p>Position value: 50 x $50 = $2,500. If the stop hits, he loses 50 x $2 = $100, exactly 1%. Notice he is putting 25% of his account into the trade but only <em>risking</em> 1%.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'position-sizing', caption: 'Start with account size and risk percent to get dollar risk. Divide by the distance from entry to stop. The result is your share count.' },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Maya has a $2,000 account and risks 1%. She plans to buy at $25 with a stop at $24. How many shares should she buy?', answer: 20, tolerance: 0, unit: '', explain: 'Dollar risk: $2,000 x 0.01 = $20. Risk per share: $25 - $24 = $1. Shares: $20 / $1 = 20. Position value: $500.' },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'position-size', props: { account: 10000, riskPct: 1, entry: 50, stop: 48 }, caption: 'Move the stop closer and watch the share count rise. Move it farther away and the share count shrinks, while the dollar risk stays the same.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Wide stop, small size. Tight stop, big size.', html: `<p>The widget shows the key insight: <strong>your dollar risk stays fixed, and the stop distance decides the size</strong>.</p><p>Same $10,000 account, same 1% ($100) risk, same $50 entry:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Stop</th><th>Risk per share</th><th>Shares</th><th>Position value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$45</td><td>$5.00</td><td>20</td><td>$1,000</td></tr><tr><td>$48</td><td>$2.00</td><td>50</td><td>$2,500</td></tr><tr><td>$49</td><td>$1.00</td><td>100</td><td>$5,000</td></tr><tr><td>$49.50</td><td>$0.50</td><td>200</td><td>$10,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A volatile stock needs a wider stop, so you buy fewer shares. A calm stock with a tight, logical stop allows more shares. Either way, being wrong costs $100.</p><p>Look at the last row though. A super-tight stop says "buy $10,000 worth," the entire account. That is a red flag, covered next.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Two trades both risk $200. Trade A has a $1 stop distance, Trade B has a $4 stop distance. Which is true?', options: ['Trade A buys 200 shares and Trade B buys 50 shares', 'Both buy 200 shares', 'Trade B buys more shares because its stop is wider', 'Trade A risks more money'], answer: 0, explain: '$200 / $1 = 200 shares. $200 / $4 = 50 shares. Same dollar risk, different size.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Where the formula can break', html: `<p>The formula assumes your stop fills at your stop price. Reality is messier:</p><ul><li><strong>Gaps.</strong> If a stock closes at $49 and opens tomorrow at $44 after bad news, your $48 stop does not save you at $48. It triggers at the open and fills near $44. Your "$100 risk" just became $250. Earnings reports are the classic gap trigger.</li><li><strong>Slippage.</strong> In fast or thinly traded stocks, a stop-market order can fill a bit worse than the stop price.</li><li><strong>Tiny stops create huge positions.</strong> As in the table, a very tight stop can tell you to put most or all of your account into one stock. Many traders add a second cap, such as never letting a single position exceed a set share of the account, so one gap cannot do outsized damage.</li><li><strong>Round down.</strong> If the math says 33.7 shares, buy 33, not 34.</li></ul><p>So the formula sets your <em>planned</em> risk. Holding through earnings, trading illiquid stocks, or using huge positions all raise your <em>real</em> risk above the plan.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "A stop is an order, not a force field. Overnight gaps jump right over it. Size for the gap, not just the stop." } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Leo has $15,000 and risks 2%. He plans to buy at $80 with a stop at $74. How many shares?', answer: 50, tolerance: 0, unit: '', explain: 'Dollar risk: $15,000 x 0.02 = $300. Risk per share: $80 - $74 = $6. Shares: $300 / $6 = 50. Position value: $4,000.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: "Ms. Ortiz sizes her bakery bets", html: `<p>Ms. Ortiz thinks about her business the same way. When she tests a new pastry, she bakes one small batch. If nobody buys it, she loses about $40 of ingredients, a tiny slice of her monthly budget. She would never bake 500 of an untested pastry. Traders call that "sizing for being wrong." She calls it common sense.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'Percent sizing adjusts itself', html: `<p>Because you risk a <em>percent</em> of the current account, your bets automatically shrink during a losing streak and grow as the account grows. At 1%, a $10,000 account risks $100. After a drawdown to $8,000, it risks $80. That built-in brake is a big reason a percent-based rule survives bad streaks so much better than betting a fixed big dollar amount every time, and why traders recalculate dollar risk from the current balance rather than the starting one.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If your position-sizing math says to buy 47.8 shares, you should round up to 48 to use your full risk budget.', answer: false, explain: 'Round down. Rounding up puts you slightly over your planned risk, and those small overages add up across many trades.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Shorts work the same way', html: `<p>If you are betting a stock will fall (a short sale), the stop sits <em>above</em> your entry, so the risk per share is <strong>Stop - Entry</strong>. Everything else is identical.</p><p>Short at $40, stop at $42: risk per share is $2. With $100 of dollar risk, that is 50 shares.</p><p>One extra warning for shorts: a stock can only fall to zero, but it can rise without limit, and gaps up on surprise news can be brutal. Short selling also requires a margin account and is generally not available to beginners or in custodial accounts. That is why many learners stick to long trades while they practice on My Desk.</p><p>Whichever side you trade, make the calculation a habit. Before every practice trade on My Desk, write the dollar risk and share count in your journal first, then compare it with the number your gut wanted. The gap between those two numbers is usually eye-opening.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'think', say: "Losses on a long are capped at what you paid. Losses on a short have no ceiling. Respect the asymmetry." } },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Sizing terms', cards: [
          { front: 'Dollar risk', back: 'Account size times risk percent. The most you plan to lose on one trade.' },
          { front: 'Risk per share', back: 'Distance from entry to stop. What each share loses if the stop hits.' },
          { front: 'Position value', back: 'Shares times entry price. How much money is in the trade, which is not the same as risk.' },
          { front: 'Gap risk', back: 'The chance price jumps past your stop, making the real loss bigger than planned.' },
          { front: 'Position cap', back: 'A maximum share of the account any single trade may use, regardless of the formula.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What is the position-sizing formula?', options: ['Shares = Account / Entry', 'Shares = (Account x Risk %) / (Entry - Stop)', 'Shares = Entry x Stop', 'Shares = Account x Risk % x Entry'], answer: 1, explain: 'Dollar risk divided by risk per share gives the share count.' },
          { q: 'Account $20,000, risk 1%, entry $100, stop $96. How many shares?', options: ['200', '20', '50', '100'], answer: 2, explain: '$20,000 x 0.01 = $200. Risk per share = $4. $200 / $4 = 50 shares.' },
          { q: 'You keep dollar risk the same but move your stop farther from entry. What happens to the share count?', options: ['It shrinks', 'It grows', 'It stays the same', 'It becomes zero'], answer: 0, explain: 'Wider stop = bigger risk per share = fewer shares for the same dollar risk.' },
          { q: 'Why can your real loss be larger than your planned risk?', options: ['Brokers charge a stop fee', 'The formula is wrong', 'Stops only work on Fridays', 'Prices can gap past your stop, so it fills at a worse price'], answer: 3, explain: 'Stops become market orders when triggered. If price gaps through, the fill can be far from your stop.' },
          { q: 'Dev\'s sizing math says to put 95% of his account into one stock because his stop is only $0.10 away. What is the wise response?', options: ['Do it; the math is the math', 'Use a cap on maximum position size and question whether such a tight stop is realistic', 'Double the position to make more', 'Remove the stop entirely'], answer: 1, explain: 'Very tight stops create oversized positions that a single gap can wreck. A max position cap protects you.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
    {
      id: 'stocks-5-stops-risk-reward',
      title: 'Stops, R-Multiples & Expectancy',
      summary: 'Where stops actually belong, how to measure trades in R, and the expectancy math that shows why win rate alone means nothing.',
      minutes: 16,
      icon: 'target',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'The stop is the trade idea', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="An order that exits a position if price reaches a set level, limiting the loss.">stop-loss</span> is not a random number you pick so the loss "doesn't hurt too much." It is the price where your trade idea is <strong>proven wrong</strong>.</p><p>If you bought because a stock bounced off support at $50, then a drop to $48.50 means support failed. Your reason for owning it is gone. That is where the stop belongs.</p><p>Bad stop placement looks like this:</p><ul><li>"I'll put it $1 below because $1 feels okay." (Ignores the chart.)</li><li>"I'll put it right at $50.00 exactly." (Where everyone else's stop sits, easy to get tagged by noise.)</li><li>"I don't use stops, I'll just watch it." (Dev's favorite. You know how that ends.)</li></ul><p>Good placement starts with the chart, then the position-size formula adjusts the share count so the dollar risk fits your rule.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: "Pick the stop from the chart, then pick the size from the stop. Never the other way around." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Two ways to place stops', html: `<p><strong>1. Structure-based.</strong> Use the market structure from chapter 4:</p><ul><li>Long trade: just below the most recent swing low or the support zone.</li><li>Breakout trade: back inside the range or below the breakout level.</li><li>Pattern trade: past the invalidation point, such as above a head and shoulders' right shoulder for a short.</li></ul><p><strong>2. Volatility-based.</strong> Use the <span class="term" data-def="Average True Range: the average size of a stock's price range per period, usually over 14 periods, including gaps.">ATR</span>, or Average True Range, another J. Welles Wilder invention. It measures how far a stock typically moves in a period, including gaps. A common approach is placing the stop 1.5 to 2 times ATR away from entry, so normal daily wiggles do not knock you out.</p><p>If a stock's 14-day ATR is $1.50, a 2-ATR stop sits $3 below entry. A jumpy stock with a $4 ATR needs much more room, which (thanks to position sizing) means fewer shares.</p><p>Many traders combine both: find the structural level, then check that it is at least about one ATR away so it is not sitting inside normal noise.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A stock has a 14-day ATR of $2.50. Maya buys at $60 and uses a 2-ATR stop. Where is her stop?', answer: 55, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '2 x $2.50 = $5. Stop = $60 - $5 = $55.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Stop orders, honestly', html: `<p>Two common types:</p><ul><li><strong>Stop (stop-market):</strong> when price hits your stop, it becomes a market order and sells at the next available price. You will get out, but maybe at a worse price in a fast drop or gap.</li><li><strong>Stop-limit:</strong> when triggered, it becomes a limit order at a price you set. You will not sell below that limit, but if price blows past it, you might <em>not get out at all</em>.</li></ul><p>For protecting against big losses, many traders prefer stop-market orders, accepting some slippage over the risk of being stuck in a collapsing position.</p><p>One golden rule: <span class="hl">never move a stop farther away to avoid taking a loss</span>. Moving it closer to lock in profit as a trade works is fine. Moving it away because you "just know" it will come back is how small planned losses become big unplanned ones.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Moving your stop away from price is not patience. It is negotiating with a loss. The loss always wins that negotiation." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Thinking in R', html: `<p>Pros measure trades in <span class="term" data-def="Your initial risk on a trade (entry minus stop, times shares). Results are measured as multiples of it.">R</span>, short for risk. 1R is the amount you planned to lose if the stop hit.</p><p>If Leo risks $100 on a trade (1R = $100):</p><ul><li>He makes $300: that is <span class="up">+3R</span>.</li><li>He gets stopped out for $100: that is <span class="down">-1R</span>.</li><li>He exits early for $50 profit: <span class="up">+0.5R</span>.</li><li>A gap hits and he loses $180: <span class="down">-1.8R</span>. (Ouch. That is why gap risk matters.)</li></ul><p>Why bother? R lets you compare trades of different sizes and accounts fairly. A $50 win on a $25 risk (+2R) was a better trade than a $200 win on a $400 risk (+0.5R), even though the second made more dollars.</p><p>The <strong>reward-to-risk ratio</strong> compares your target to your stop before entry. Entry $50, stop $48, target $56: risk $2, reward $6, so 3:1, or a 3R target.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'happy', say: "R turns every trade into the same unit. Big account, small account, $5 stock, $500 stock. Apples to apples." } },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'risk-reward', props: { entry: 50, stop: 48, target: 56 }, caption: 'Drag the stop and target. Watch the reward-to-risk ratio change, and notice the minimum win rate needed to break even.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Win rate is half the story', html: `<p>People love bragging about win rate. "I win 80% of my trades!" Great. How big are the losses?</p><p>A trader who wins 80% of the time but makes $1 per win and loses $5 per loss is slowly going broke. A trader who wins only 35% of the time but makes $3 per win and loses $1 per loss can be doing well.</p><p>The minimum win rate needed to break even (before costs) depends on your reward-to-risk ratio:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Reward : risk</th><th>Break-even win rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 : 1</td><td>50%</td></tr><tr><td>2 : 1</td><td>about 33%</td></tr><tr><td>3 : 1</td><td>25%</td></tr><tr><td>1 : 2</td><td>about 67%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The formula is <strong>1 / (1 + reward/risk)</strong>. Higher payoffs let you be wrong more often. But targets far away also get hit less often, so you cannot just set a 10:1 target and celebrate. Win rate and payoff pull against each other.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'A strategy targets 2R and risks 1R on every trade. Ignoring costs, roughly what win rate does it need to break even?', options: ['About 33%', '50%', '67%', '20%'], answer: 0, explain: '1 / (1 + 2) = 1/3, about 33%. Win a third of the time at +2R and lose two thirds at -1R and you net zero.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Expectancy: the number that matters', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="The average amount you expect to win or lose per trade over many trades.">Expectancy</span> combines win rate and payoff into one number: what you make <em>on average per trade</em> over many trades.</p><p><span class="hl">Expectancy = (Win % x Average win) - (Loss % x Average loss)</span></p><p>Leo's last 50 paper trades: he won 40% of them with an average win of $250, and lost 60% with an average loss of $100.</p><p>(0.40 x $250) - (0.60 x $100) = $100 - $60 = <strong>+$40 per trade</strong>.</p><p>In R terms: average win +2.5R, average loss -1R, so expectancy = 0.40 x 2.5 - 0.60 x 1 = <strong>+0.4R</strong> per trade.</p><p>Two big cautions. First, subtract costs: spreads and slippage eat into small edges fast. Second, 50 trades is a small sample. Expectancy estimates get trustworthy only over many trades, and they can change when market conditions change.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'A strategy wins 30% of the time with an average win of $400, and loses 70% of the time with an average loss of $150. What is its expectancy per trade?', answer: 15, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: '(0.30 x $400) - (0.70 x $150) = $120 - $105 = +$15 per trade. Positive, but thin, so costs could erase it.' },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'A strategy with a 75% win rate must have positive expectancy.', answer: false, explain: 'If the average loss is much bigger than the average win, a high win rate can still lose money. Example: win $1 three times, lose $5 once = -$2.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Plan the whole trade before you click', html: `<p>Before any trade on My Desk, write four numbers: <strong>entry, stop, target, share count</strong>. Then calculate the reward-to-risk ratio. If it is under about 1.5 to 1 and you have no strong reason for a high win rate, consider skipping it. Rule-based bots like Bolt only take trades that pass a written plan; you can hold yourself to the same standard.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Where does a structure-based stop belong on a long trade bought at support?', options: ['Exactly at your entry price', 'Just below the support zone or recent swing low', 'At a round number above entry', 'Wherever the loss feels comfortable'], answer: 1, explain: 'The stop goes where the trade idea is proven wrong: below the support that justified the entry.' },
          { q: 'You risk $200 (1R) and close the trade with a $500 profit. What is the R-multiple?', options: ['+5R', '+2R', '+0.4R', '+2.5R'], answer: 3, explain: '$500 / $200 = 2.5, so +2.5R.' },
          { q: 'What is a key risk of a stop-limit order compared with a stop-market order?', options: ['It might not fill if price gaps past the limit', 'It always fills at a worse price', 'It cannot be canceled', 'It doubles your position'], answer: 0, explain: 'A stop-limit will not sell below your limit, so in a fast drop you could stay stuck in the position.' },
          { q: 'Win rate 50%, average win $200, average loss $100. What is the expectancy per trade?', options: ['+$100', '+$150', '+$50', '-$50'], answer: 2, explain: '(0.5 x 200) - (0.5 x 100) = 100 - 50 = +$50.' },
          { q: 'Dev\'s trade is going against him, so he moves his stop from $48 down to $44 "to give it room." What is the problem?', options: ['Nothing, flexibility is good', 'He turned a planned 1R loss into a potentially much larger, unplanned loss', 'Stops cannot be moved', 'He should have moved it to $49'], answer: 1, explain: 'Widening a stop to avoid a loss breaks the risk plan. Tightening to protect gains is fine; widening is not.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
    {
      id: 'stocks-5-trading-styles',
      title: 'Day, Swing, Position or Invest?',
      summary: 'Four ways to participate in the stock market, compared honestly on time, costs, taxes, rules and odds, plus how the site\'s bot strategies fit each one.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'clock',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Same market, different games', html: `<p>Everyone in the stock market is buying and selling the same shares, but they are playing very different games depending on <strong>how long they hold</strong>:</p><ul><li><span class="term" data-def="Buying and selling within the same trading day, with no positions held overnight.">Day trading</span>: in and out the same day. Positions are closed before the close.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="Holding trades for a few days to a few weeks to capture a single price swing.">Swing trading</span>: holding for days to a few weeks to catch one swing.</li><li><span class="term" data-def="Holding trades for weeks to months to ride a larger trend.">Position trading</span>: holding for weeks to months to ride a bigger trend.</li><li><strong>Long-term investing</strong>: holding for years, often decades, betting on business growth and compounding rather than price swings.</li></ul><p>None of these is automatically "right." But they differ hugely in time commitment, costs, taxes, stress and, importantly, in how the odds tend to work out for regular people.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Pick the game that fits your life, not the one that looks coolest on social media." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Time: the hidden price tag', html: `<p>Each style asks for a very different amount of your attention:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Typical hold</th><th>Screen time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Day trading</td><td>Minutes to hours</td><td>Hours every market day</td></tr><tr><td>Swing trading</td><td>Days to weeks</td><td>About an hour a day</td></tr><tr><td>Position trading</td><td>Weeks to months</td><td>A few hours a week</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term investing</td><td>Years</td><td>A few hours a year</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Day trading US stocks mostly happens during regular hours, 9:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m. ET, which is exactly when Maya is in school and Leo is at work. Trying to day trade from a phone under the desk is a recipe for rushed, sloppy decisions.</p><p>Swing and position trading can fit around a job because decisions can be made in the evening, with orders (including stops) placed in advance. Long-term investing fits around basically anything.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each style to its typical holding period.', pairs: [
          { left: 'Day trading', right: 'Closed before the market close' },
          { left: 'Swing trading', right: 'A few days to a few weeks' },
          { left: 'Position trading', right: 'Weeks to months' },
          { left: 'Long-term investing', right: 'Years or decades' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Costs: commission-free is not cost-free', html: `<p>Most big US brokers now charge $0 commission on online stock trades. That does not make trading free. Every round trip still costs you:</p><ul><li><strong>The spread.</strong> You buy near the ask and sell near the bid. On a liquid stock that might be a penny; on a small, thinly traded stock it can be much more.</li><li><strong>Slippage.</strong> Market orders and stops in fast markets can fill worse than the price you saw.</li><li><strong>Small regulatory fees</strong> on sales.</li></ul><p>For a long-term investor making a handful of trades a year, these are tiny. For a day trader making 10 trades a day, they compound into a serious drag. If each round trip costs even 0.05% in spread and slippage, 10 round trips a day is 0.5% of the traded amount per day, over 100% a year if you traded your whole account each time. That cost is certain; the profits are not.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "More trades means more tiny costs. Tiny costs times thousands of trades is not tiny." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Taxes change the math', html: `<p>In the US, how long you hold matters a lot at tax time (in a regular taxable account):</p><ul><li><strong>Short-term capital gains</strong> (held one year or less) are taxed at your ordinary income tax rate, the same as wages.</li><li><strong>Long-term capital gains</strong> (held more than one year) get lower rates, currently 0%, 15% or 20% for most people depending on income.</li></ul><p>Day, swing and position trades are almost always short-term. Long-term investing can qualify for the lower rates, and gains are not taxed at all until you sell.</p><p>There is also the <span class="term" data-def="A US tax rule: if you sell at a loss and buy the same or a substantially identical security within 30 days before or after, you cannot claim the loss right away.">wash sale rule</span>: sell at a loss and buy the same stock back within 30 days before or after, and you cannot deduct that loss yet. Active traders bump into it constantly.</p><p>Tax rules vary by country and change over time. Treat this as the general idea and check current rules or talk to a tax professional before relying on it.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Leo sells shares he held for 8 months at a profit in a regular taxable account. In the US, how is that gain generally treated?', options: ['As a short-term gain taxed at his ordinary income rate', 'As a long-term gain at a lower rate', 'It is tax-free', 'It is only taxed if he buys again within 30 days'], answer: 0, explain: 'Held one year or less = short-term. It is taxed like ordinary income. He would need to hold more than one year for long-term rates.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The pattern day trader rule', html: `<p>For years, US day traders have had to deal with FINRA's <span class="term" data-def="A long-standing FINRA rule that treats margin accounts making 4 or more day trades in 5 business days as pattern day traders, requiring $25,000 in equity.">pattern day trader</span> (PDT) rule. Under the long-standing version:</p><ul><li>If you make <strong>four or more day trades within five business days</strong> in a <strong>margin account</strong> (and those trades are more than 6% of your trades in that window), you are labeled a pattern day trader.</li><li>Pattern day traders must keep at least <strong>$25,000</strong> in account equity to keep day trading. Fall below and day trading gets restricted.</li></ul><p>Here is the important part: <span class="hl">this rule has been under revision</span>. FINRA has been working on replacing the fixed $25,000 test with a different approach to intraday margin. So the exact rule may be different by the time you read this. Check FINRA's website or your broker for the current requirements before you plan around it.</p><p>Also: if you are under 18, you generally cannot open your own brokerage account. Custodial accounts, run by an adult for you, typically do not allow margin.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The odds, honestly', html: `<p>Day trading is the style most heavily marketed with flashy screenshots and the one with the worst track record for regular people. Large studies of individual day traders, including research using complete trading records from Taiwan and from Brazil, have found that the large majority lost money after costs, and only a tiny fraction earned consistent profits over time.</p><p>Why? Day traders compete against professional firms with faster data, better execution and lower costs. Costs pile up with every trade. And the fast pace amplifies every emotional mistake you will meet in the next lesson.</p><p>Meanwhile, plain long-term investing in broad, diversified index funds has historically rewarded patience over long periods, though with real drops along the way and no guarantees about the future.</p><p>That does not mean you cannot learn short-term trading. It means: learn it with paper money and tiny real stakes, measure your results honestly, and keep your long-term savings out of the experiment.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Research on real day traders' records is sobering: most lost money. Study the data before you study the setups." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'Because most brokers charge $0 commission, frequent day trading has essentially no costs.', answer: false, explain: 'Spreads, slippage and small regulatory fees still apply on every trade. They add up quickly when you trade many times a day.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Where the bot strategies fit', html: `<p>The strategies Bolt can run on the AI Trader map roughly onto these styles:</p><ul><li><strong>Day trading:</strong> Opening Range Breakout and Gap and Go (both focus on the first part of the session), VWAP Mean Reversion (VWAP resets daily), and EMA Crossover (9/21) when it runs on intraday charts.</li><li><strong>Swing trading:</strong> RSI Swing, Bollinger Squeeze and Momentum Breakout, which look for moves lasting days to weeks.</li><li><strong>Position trading:</strong> CANSLIM, which looks to ride leading growth stocks for weeks to months after a breakout.</li><li><strong>Long-term investing:</strong> Quality Compounder, which screens on business fundamentals rather than short-term chart signals.</li></ul><p>Exact holding times depend on each strategy's rule settings, so check the rule card for each one. Running two or three side by side on My Desk for a month is a great way to feel the difference in pace, trade count and stress, without risking a cent.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'happy', say: "I can run fast strategies all day without getting tired or tilted. You are not a robot. Choose accordingly." } },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Order these styles from shortest to longest typical holding period.', items: ['Day trading', 'Swing trading', 'Position trading', 'Long-term investing'], explain: 'Day trades close the same day, swings last days to weeks, position trades weeks to months, and investments years.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'A sensible split', html: `<p>Plenty of people do both: a long-term investing core in diversified funds, plus a small, strictly limited amount for active trading they treat as tuition. The last lesson in this chapter covers exactly how to set that up, so a trading mistake can never touch your real savings.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which style typically holds positions for a few days to a few weeks?', options: ['Day trading', 'Long-term investing', 'Swing trading', 'Index investing'], answer: 2, explain: 'Swing traders try to capture a single swing over days to weeks.' },
          { q: 'Under the long-standing PDT rule, which situation flags an account as a pattern day trader?', options: ['Four or more day trades in five business days in a margin account', 'Any day trade in a cash account', 'Holding a stock for more than one year', 'One day trade per month'], answer: 0, explain: '4+ day trades in 5 business days in a margin account (above 6% of trades). The rule has been under revision, so check the current version.' },
          { q: 'In the US, gains on a stock held more than one year in a taxable account are generally...', options: ['Taxed at ordinary income rates', 'Never taxed', 'Taxed only if over $25,000', 'Taxed at lower long-term capital gains rates'], answer: 3, explain: 'Long-term capital gains rates (currently 0%, 15% or 20% for most people) apply to holdings over one year.' },
          { q: 'What have large studies of individual day traders generally found?', options: ['Most were consistently profitable', 'The large majority lost money after costs', 'Results were identical to index investing', 'Day traders had lower costs than investors'], answer: 1, explain: 'Studies using full trading records, such as from Taiwan and Brazil, found most day traders lost money and few profited consistently.' },
          { q: 'Which of Bolt\'s strategies is best matched to long-term investing?', options: ['Gap and Go', 'Opening Range Breakout', 'Quality Compounder', 'VWAP Mean Reversion'], answer: 2, explain: 'Quality Compounder screens on business fundamentals for long holding periods. The others are intraday strategies.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
    {
      id: 'stocks-5-your-brain',
      title: 'Your Brain on Markets',
      summary: 'FOMO, loss aversion, revenge trading, overconfidence and more. Meet the biases that wreck good plans, and Dev, who has met all of them.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'warning',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Meet the real opponent', html: `<p>You have learned charts, indicators, stops and sizing. Here is the uncomfortable truth: most people who lose money trading do not lose because they lacked information. They lose because <strong>they did not follow their own plan</strong>.</p><p>Human brains evolved to survive on the savanna, not to manage a brokerage account. The same instincts that kept our ancestors alive (run with the crowd, avoid losses at all costs, act fast when excited) are almost perfectly designed to make you buy high, sell low and trade too much.</p><p>Psychologists call these mental shortcuts <span class="term" data-def="Predictable patterns where human thinking departs from logic, often in the same direction for most people.">cognitive biases</span>. You cannot delete them. You can learn their names, spot them in action, and build rules that stop them from pressing the buy button for you.</p><p>Our guide for this lesson is Dev. He has fallen for every single one.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'think', say: "The market is not your biggest risk. The person holding your phone is." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'FOMO: the crowd is buying', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Fear of missing out: the anxious urge to jump into something because others seem to be profiting from it.">FOMO</span>, fear of missing out, hits when a stock is ripping and everyone online seems to be getting rich.</p><p>Dev sees a stock up 40% in two days. Group chats are full of rocket memes. He has no plan, no stop and no idea why it is moving. He buys anyway, near the top of the spike, because sitting out feels physically painful. The next morning it gives back half the move.</p><p>FOMO pushes you to buy <em>after</em> the move, when risk is highest and reward is smallest. It also makes you skip every step of the process: no level, no stop, no sizing.</p><p><strong>Defense:</strong> a written rule, like "I only enter at planned levels with a stop, and if I missed it, I missed it." There will always be another setup. Missing a trade costs you nothing. Chasing one can cost a lot.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Loss aversion and the disposition effect', html: `<p>Research by psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky found that people feel losses much more intensely than equal gains. In many experiments, losing $100 feels roughly twice as bad as winning $100 feels good. This is <span class="term" data-def="The tendency to feel losses more strongly than gains of the same size.">loss aversion</span>.</p><p>In trading it shows up as the <span class="term" data-def="The habit of selling winning positions too early and holding losing positions too long.">disposition effect</span>: selling winners too early (to lock in that good feeling) and holding losers too long (because selling makes the loss "real"). Studies of real brokerage accounts have found exactly this pattern.</p><p>Dev sells a stock up 6% the moment it turns green. Meanwhile he holds another down 35% because "it's not a loss until I sell." Spoiler: it is a loss either way. The account balance does not care whether you clicked sell.</p><p><strong>Defense:</strong> set the stop and target before entering, then let the orders execute. Ask: "If I had cash instead of this stock today, would I buy it here?" If not, why hold it?</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: "Hoo-man brains feel a $100 loss about twice as hard as a $100 win. Evolution was not designed for brokerage apps." } },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Maya refuses to sell a stock that is down 30% because "it isn\'t a real loss until I sell." Which bias is this?', options: ['FOMO', 'Anchoring', 'Loss aversion and the disposition effect', 'Overconfidence'], answer: 2, explain: 'Holding losers to avoid the pain of "making it real" is the disposition effect, driven by loss aversion. The loss already exists.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Revenge trading', html: `<p>Dev just took a $300 loss. He is angry. He wants it back <em>right now</em>. So he takes another trade immediately, twice the normal size, on a setup he would usually skip. It loses too. Now he is down $900 and furious.</p><p>That is <span class="term" data-def="Taking impulsive, often oversized trades right after a loss to try to win the money back quickly.">revenge trading</span>, sometimes called being "on tilt," a term borrowed from poker. It turns one normal loss into a disaster. The market does not know you lost money and does not owe you anything.</p><p><strong>Defenses that actually work:</strong></p><ul><li>A daily loss limit, such as stopping for the day after losing 2% or 3% of the account.</li><li>A cool-off rule: after two losses in a row, walk away for at least an hour.</li><li>Never increase size after a loss. If anything, shrink it.</li></ul><p>These rules feel unnecessary when you are calm. They are written for the version of you who is not.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Overconfidence', html: `<p>Dev had a great month. Five winners in a row. He decides he has "figured out the market," triples his size and starts trading more often.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="Believing your knowledge, skill or odds of success are better than they really are.">Overconfidence</span> is the belief that your skill is higher than it is. A short winning streak is often just luck in a rising market, but it feels like genius.</p><p>A well-known study by Brad Barber and Terrance Odean looked at tens of thousands of US brokerage households in the 1990s. Those who traded the most earned noticeably lower returns than those who traded the least, largely because trading costs ate their gains. More activity, driven by confidence, made results worse.</p><p><strong>Defense:</strong> judge yourself over a large number of trades, not a hot week. Keep size fixed by your risk rule, no matter how sure you feel. Feeling certain is not data.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'warn', say: "A rising tide lifts all boats. That doesn't make you a great sailor." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'After five winning trades in a row, increasing your position size is justified because you have proven your skill.', answer: false, explain: 'Five trades is far too small a sample to separate skill from luck. Raising size because of a streak is classic overconfidence.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Confirmation bias, sunk cost and anchoring', html: `<ul><li><strong>Confirmation bias:</strong> Dev owns a stock, so he only reads bullish posts about it and scrolls past anything negative. He is not researching; he is collecting cheerleaders. <em>Defense:</em> write down the strongest argument <em>against</em> your trade before you take it.</li><li><strong><span class="term" data-def="Continuing something because of what you already put in, even when the future outlook says to stop.">Sunk cost fallacy</span>:</strong> "I've already lost $2,000 on this, I can't sell now." The $2,000 is gone either way. The only question is whether this is the best place for the money you have <em>now</em>. <em>Defense:</em> judge positions on future prospects, not past pain.</li><li><strong><span class="term" data-def="Relying too heavily on the first number you saw, such as a past price, when making decisions.">Anchoring</span>:</strong> A stock once traded at $200. Now it is $60, and Dev thinks "it's so cheap, it used to be $200." That old price is an anchor, not a valuation. The business may have changed completely. <em>Defense:</em> ignore old prices; ask what the company is worth today.</li></ul>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each thought to the bias behind it.', pairs: [
          { left: '"Everyone is making money on this, I have to get in now!"', right: 'FOMO' },
          { left: '"I\'ll win it back on the next trade, double size."', right: 'Revenge trading' },
          { left: '"It used to be $200, so $60 is a steal."', right: 'Anchoring' },
          { left: '"I\'ve put too much into this to sell now."', right: 'Sunk cost fallacy' },
          { left: '"Five wins in a row. I\'ve cracked it."', right: 'Overconfidence' },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Rules beat willpower', html: `<p>Bolt the bot has no FOMO, no ego and no revenge streak. That is not because it is smart; it is because it follows written rules. You can borrow that advantage: write your entry rules, stop rules, size rule and daily loss limit, and keep them visible while you trade. When your feelings and your rules disagree, the rules were written by the calmer version of you.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Bias field guide', cards: [
          { front: 'FOMO', back: 'Chasing a move because others seem to be profiting. Defense: only planned entries.' },
          { front: 'Loss aversion', back: 'Losses feel worse than equal gains. Leads to holding losers. Defense: preset stops.' },
          { front: 'Revenge trading', back: 'Impulsive trades to win back a loss. Defense: daily loss limit and cool-off.' },
          { front: 'Overconfidence', back: 'Overrating your skill after a streak. Defense: fixed sizing, big samples.' },
          { front: 'Sunk cost', back: 'Holding on because of past losses. Defense: judge only the future.' },
          { front: 'Anchoring', back: 'Fixating on an old price. Defense: value it as it is today.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which bias makes people sell winners too early and hold losers too long?', options: ['Anchoring', 'FOMO', 'Overconfidence', 'The disposition effect, driven by loss aversion'], answer: 3, explain: 'Locking in gains feels good and realizing losses feels bad, so people do the reverse of "cut losses, let winners run."' },
          { q: 'Dev loses $300 and immediately takes a double-size trade to win it back. What is this?', options: ['Revenge trading', 'Position sizing', 'Dollar-cost averaging', 'Mean reversion'], answer: 0, explain: 'Impulsive, oversized trading after a loss is revenge trading, or being on tilt.' },
          { q: 'What did Barber and Odean\'s study of US brokerage households find?', options: ['Frequent traders earned the highest returns', 'Trading frequency had no effect', 'Households that traded most earned noticeably lower returns than those that traded least', 'Only professional traders were studied'], answer: 2, explain: 'The most active traders underperformed, largely because of trading costs. Overconfidence leads to overtrading.' },
          { q: 'Which is the best defense against revenge trading?', options: ['Increasing size after losses', 'A preset daily loss limit and a cool-off rule after losses', 'Following more social media accounts', 'Trading only meme stocks'], answer: 1, explain: 'Rules written in advance stop the tilted version of you from making things worse.' },
          { q: 'Leo thinks a stock is a bargain at $40 because it traded at $120 two years ago. Which bias is at work?', options: ['Anchoring', 'Sunk cost', 'Revenge trading', 'FOMO'], answer: 0, explain: 'The old $120 price is an anchor. Whether $40 is cheap depends on the business today, not past prices.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
    {
      id: 'stocks-5-journal-backtest-paper',
      title: 'Journal, Backtest, Paper Trade',
      summary: 'How to test a strategy without fooling yourself: what to log, the traps in backtesting, why paper results flatter you, and a checklist before using real money.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'book',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Your memory is a terrible accountant', html: `<p>Ask Dev how his trading is going and he will tell you about the stock that doubled. He will not mention the eleven trades that lost money. He is not lying. Human memory just keeps the highlights and quietly deletes the rest.</p><p>That is why serious traders keep a <span class="term" data-def="A written record of every trade: the plan, the result, and what you were thinking and feeling.">trading journal</span>. It is the only honest record of whether your approach actually works. Without one, you are guessing about your own results.</p><p>A journal also turns losses into lessons. A single loss teaches you almost nothing, since even great setups fail. But twenty journal entries showing you always lose on trades taken in the first five minutes of the day? That is a pattern you can fix.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'penny', mood: 'happy', say: "Banks track every cent. Your trading deserves the same respect. Write it all down." } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'What to log', html: `<p>For every trade, real or paper, record:</p><ul><li><strong>The basics:</strong> date, time, ticker, long or short.</li><li><strong>The plan:</strong> setup name, entry, stop, target, share count, dollar risk (1R).</li><li><strong>The reason:</strong> one or two sentences on why. "Bull flag on daily, breakout on 2x volume, above rising 50-day."</li><li><strong>The result:</strong> exit price, profit or loss in dollars and in R.</li><li><strong>The execution:</strong> did you follow the plan? Did you move the stop, exit early, or chase the entry?</li><li><strong>Your state:</strong> calm, bored, excited, angry, tired. This column is often the most revealing.</li><li><strong>A chart screenshot</strong> saved alongside the entry.</li></ul><p>Then review weekly. Sort by setup and add up R for each. Sort by emotional state. Look for the trades where you broke your rules. Most traders discover their losses cluster in a few repeatable mistakes, which is great news, because those are fixable.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Maya\'s journal shows five trades: +2R, -1R, -1R, +3R, -1R. What is her total in R?', answer: 2, tolerance: 0, unit: '', explain: '2 - 1 - 1 + 3 - 1 = +2R. She lost three of five trades and still came out ahead because her winners were bigger than her losers.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Backtesting: testing on the past', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Applying a strategy's exact rules to historical price data to see how it would have performed.">Backtesting</span> means running your strategy's exact rules over historical data to see how it <em>would have</em> done. It can save you months: a rule that would have lost money for ten years straight probably will not suddenly start working.</p><p>But backtests are famously easy to fool yourself with. The big traps:</p><ul><li><strong>Overfitting:</strong> tweaking settings until the past looks perfect. "Buy when RSI is below 27.3 on Tuesdays, but only if the 17-day EMA is rising" fits history beautifully and then falls apart on new data. You did not find an edge; you memorized noise.</li><li><strong>Look-ahead bias:</strong> accidentally using information that was not available at the time, like a day's closing price to decide a trade made at the open, or earnings figures before they were released.</li><li><strong>Survivorship bias:</strong> testing only on companies that exist today, ignoring the ones that went bankrupt or were delisted. That makes almost any buy strategy look better.</li><li><strong>Ignoring costs:</strong> leaving out spreads and slippage, which can erase a thin edge.</li></ul>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'warn', say: "Give me enough settings and I can fit any past perfectly. That does not mean I can predict tomorrow. Fewer knobs, more trust." } },
        { type: 'match', prompt: 'Match each backtesting trap to an example.', pairs: [
          { left: 'Overfitting', right: 'Tuning 12 settings until past returns look amazing' },
          { left: 'Look-ahead bias', right: 'Using the day\'s close to decide a trade at the open' },
          { left: 'Survivorship bias', right: 'Testing only on companies that still exist today' },
          { left: 'Ignoring costs', right: 'Assuming every trade fills at the exact price with no spread' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Out-of-sample: the honest check', html: `<p>The best defense against overfitting is to hold some data back.</p><ol><li>Build and tune the rules on one chunk of history, the <strong>in-sample</strong> period, say 2015 to 2021.</li><li>Then run the <em>finished, untouched</em> rules on a later period you never looked at, the <strong>out-of-sample</strong> period, say 2022 onward.</li></ol><p>If results collapse out-of-sample, the strategy was probably overfit. If they hold up reasonably (usually somewhat worse than in-sample, which is normal), that is a better sign.</p><p>Other healthy habits: prefer simple rules with few settings, test across many stocks and different market conditions (rising, falling, sideways), and make sure you have a decent number of trades. Ten trades tell you almost nothing. A few hundred start to mean something.</p><p>Even a good backtest is a sketch of the past, not a promise about the future. Markets change.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'A strategy returned 80% a year from 2015 to 2021 after heavy tuning, then lost money on 2022-2024 data it had never seen. What is the most likely explanation?', options: ['The strategy was overfit to the in-sample period', 'The out-of-sample data was wrong', 'Markets closed in 2022', 'The strategy needs even more settings'], answer: 0, explain: 'Great in-sample results that collapse on fresh data are the classic sign of overfitting. More tuning would make it worse.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why paper trading flatters you', html: `<p>Paper trading on My Desk is a fantastic way to practice: real prices, real rules, zero risk. But paper results usually look <strong>better</strong> than real results would. Here is why:</p><ul><li><strong>Perfect fills.</strong> Simulators often fill your order at the price shown. In real markets, especially fast ones, you get slippage, partial fills, and limit orders that do not fill even when price touches your level because others were ahead of you in line.</li><li><strong>No emotions.</strong> Taking a $500 paper loss feels like nothing. Taking a real $500 loss makes your hands shake. Real money brings back every bias from the last lesson: hesitating on entries, moving stops, revenge trading.</li><li><strong>Bigger sizes feel easy.</strong> It is easy to "trade" $100,000 in paper money. Real decisions get harder as the amount grows.</li></ul><p>So treat paper results as an <span class="hl">upper limit</span>. If a strategy barely breaks even on paper, it will probably lose with real money.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: "Paper money has no heartbeat. Real money does. Expect your live results to be worse than your paper results, at least at first." } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'If a strategy is slightly profitable in paper trading, it will be at least as profitable with real money.', answer: false, explain: 'Real trading adds slippage, imperfect fills and emotional mistakes. A slim paper edge often disappears live.' },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Testing vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Trading journal', back: 'A written record of every trade: plan, result, and your state of mind.' },
          { front: 'Backtest', back: 'Running exact rules over historical data to see how they would have done.' },
          { front: 'Overfitting', back: 'Tuning rules so tightly to past data that they fail on new data.' },
          { front: 'Out-of-sample', back: 'Data held back from tuning and used only for a final honest check.' },
          { front: 'Slippage', back: 'The gap between the price you expected and the price you actually got.' },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Before going live: a checklist', html: `<ol><li>You have <strong>written rules</strong> for entry, stop, target, sizing and a daily loss limit.</li><li>You have a <strong>meaningful sample</strong> of paper trades (many traders aim for at least 50 to 100) logged in your journal.</li><li>Expectancy is <strong>positive after estimated costs</strong>, not just before.</li><li>You <strong>followed your rules</strong> on nearly every paper trade. If you could not follow them with fake money, real money will be harder.</li><li>You will start with <strong>tiny size</strong>, smaller than your rules allow, using money you can truly afford to lose and that is separate from savings.</li><li>If you are under 18, an adult who runs your custodial account is part of the decision.</li></ol>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: 'Put these steps for developing a strategy in a sensible order.', items: ['Write clear, specific rules', 'Backtest on in-sample historical data', 'Check results on out-of-sample data', 'Paper trade it live and journal every trade', 'Go live with very small size'], explain: 'Each step is a cheaper filter than the next. Ideas that fail early cost you nothing; ideas that fail live cost real money.' },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'Which journal column often reveals the most about a trader\'s mistakes?', options: ['The ticker symbol', 'The day of the week', 'Their emotional state and whether they followed the plan', 'The stock\'s logo'], answer: 2, explain: 'Rule breaks and emotional states tend to explain losses better than anything else.' },
          { q: 'A backtest uses each day\'s closing price to decide trades placed at that day\'s open. This is...', options: ['Survivorship bias', 'Look-ahead bias', 'Out-of-sample testing', 'Position sizing'], answer: 1, explain: 'The close was not known at the open. Using future information inflates results.' },
          { q: 'Why does testing only on stocks that exist today make results look too good?', options: ['It ignores companies that failed or were delisted', 'Current stocks have lower volume', 'Old data is always wrong', 'It increases trading costs'], answer: 0, explain: 'That is survivorship bias: the losers have been quietly removed from the test.' },
          { q: 'Which is NOT a reason paper trading results tend to look better than live results?', options: ['Fills are often perfect in simulation', 'There is no fear or greed with fake money', 'Limit orders may fill in simulation when they would not live', 'Real stocks move in different directions than paper stocks'], answer: 3, explain: 'Paper trading uses real prices. The difference comes from fills, slippage and emotions, not different price moves.' },
          { q: 'Dev wants to go live after 6 paper trades, 5 of them winners. What is the biggest issue?', options: ['Six trades is far too small a sample to judge a strategy', 'He won too many', 'Paper trading is illegal', 'He should have used more indicators'], answer: 0, explain: 'A handful of trades can easily be luck. Build a large logged sample with positive expectancy after costs first.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
    {
      id: 'stocks-5-portfolio-rebalancing',
      title: 'Building & Rebalancing a Portfolio',
      summary: 'Core and satellite, matching your mix to your goals and timeline, rebalancing by calendar or bands, and the hidden danger of concentration.',
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'briefcase',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'From trades to a plan', html: `<p>Everything so far has been about individual trades. But your money as a whole is a <span class="term" data-def="The full collection of everything you own as investments: stocks, funds, bonds, cash and more.">portfolio</span>, and how you build it matters more than any single trade.</p><p>A portfolio answers three questions:</p><ol><li><strong>What is this money for?</strong> A car in two years? College in five? Retirement in forty?</li><li><strong>How should it be split</strong> between stocks, bonds, cash and other assets?</li><li><strong>How will you keep it on track</strong> as markets move it around?</li></ol><p>Notice none of those questions is "which stock is going up next week?" The biggest drivers of long-term results for most people are the overall mix, costs, and staying invested through rough patches, not picking the perfect trade.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'penny', mood: 'think', say: "Every dollar needs a job and a deadline. Money for next summer and money for age 65 should not live in the same place." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'asset-classes', caption: 'Stocks, bonds, cash, real estate and commodities behave differently. Mixing them is the starting point of asset allocation.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Asset allocation by goal and time', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="How your money is split among asset types such as stocks, bonds and cash.">Asset allocation</span> is your mix, and the biggest input is <strong>time horizon</strong>: when you will need the money.</p><ul><li><strong>Short-term (within a few years):</strong> money for a car, tuition next fall, or an emergency fund generally belongs in safer places like high-yield savings or short-term Treasuries. Stocks can drop 30% or more in a bad year, and you do not want to be forced to sell at the bottom.</li><li><strong>Medium-term (around 3 to 10 years):</strong> often a blend of stocks and bonds, with more safety as the date gets close.</li><li><strong>Long-term (10+ years):</strong> more room for stocks, because there is time to recover from crashes. Historically, broad stock markets have recovered from their big declines over long periods, though some recoveries took many years.</li></ul><p>Then adjust for <strong>risk tolerance</strong>: how big a drop you can sit through without panic-selling. A plan you abandon in a crash is worse than a slightly more conservative plan you stick with.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: 'Leo is saving for a house down payment he plans to make in 18 months. Which approach fits best?', options: ['Put it all in a single growth stock', 'Keep it mostly in safer places like high-yield savings or short-term Treasuries', 'Day trade it to grow it faster', 'Put it all in a broad stock index fund'], answer: 1, explain: 'With a short horizon, a stock market drop could leave him short right when he needs the money. Safety matters more than growth here.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Core and satellite', html: `<p>A popular structure for people who want to invest <em>and</em> do some active trading is <span class="term" data-def="A portfolio built mostly from broad, low-cost diversified funds (the core), plus a small portion for individual picks or active strategies (the satellites).">core and satellite</span>:</p><ul><li><strong>The core</strong>, the large majority of the money: broad, low-cost, diversified funds, such as total-market or large index funds, maybe plus bond funds depending on your horizon. Boring, cheap, and designed to capture the market's long-run return.</li><li><strong>The satellites</strong>, a small slice: individual stocks you researched, a strategy you are testing, a sector you believe in. Many people keep this to something like 5% to 10%, though the exact number is a personal choice.</li></ul><p>The beauty of this setup: if a satellite blows up (and while learning, some will), your core is untouched. Your long-term plan survives your experiments.</p><p>Leo puts most of his monthly investing into index funds in his retirement and brokerage accounts, and keeps a small separate trading account he treats as tuition. Even if he lost the whole trading account, his future would be fine.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: "Boring core, fun satellites. The core pays for your future. The satellites pay for your education." } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'diversification', caption: 'One stock can swing wildly. A basket of many stocks smooths the ride, because their ups and downs partly cancel out.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Concentration risk', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="The danger of having too much of your money in one stock, sector or asset, so a single failure does major damage.">Concentration risk</span> is having too much riding on one thing. Single stocks can and do collapse: frauds, failed products, disruption, bankruptcy. Even great companies have fallen 50% or more at some point.</p><p>A famous cautionary tale: when Enron collapsed in 2001, many of its employees had much of their retirement savings in Enron stock. They lost their jobs and their savings at the same time.</p><p>Concentration sneaks in quietly:</p><ul><li>One winner grows until it is half your portfolio.</li><li>You own five "different" funds that all hold the same big tech names.</li><li>You hold lots of your employer's stock, so your paycheck and your savings depend on the same company.</li></ul><p>Concentration can create huge wins too, which is why it is tempting. But a portfolio is supposed to get you to your goals reliably, and spreading money out is one of the few genuinely free risk reducers in investing.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Drift and rebalancing', html: `<p>Say Maya sets a 60% stocks / 40% bonds mix. A year later stocks have rallied and bonds have not. Now she is at 68% / 32%. Her portfolio has <strong>drifted</strong> to more risk than she chose.</p><p><span class="term" data-def="Selling some of what has grown and buying what has lagged to bring your mix back to its target.">Rebalancing</span> pulls it back: trim what has grown, add to what has lagged. It feels backward (selling winners, buying laggards), but it keeps your risk level where you intended and enforces "sell high, buy low" mechanically.</p><p>Two common methods:</p><ul><li><strong>Calendar:</strong> rebalance on a set schedule, such as once a year. Simple and hard to forget.</li><li><strong>Bands (thresholds):</strong> rebalance only when an asset drifts beyond a set range, such as 5 percentage points from its target. It reacts to big moves and ignores small ones.</li></ul><p>Many people combine them: check on a schedule, act only if outside the band. Rebalancing too often mostly adds costs and taxes.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: 'Maya\'s target is 60% stocks. Her $11,000 portfolio now holds $7,500 in stocks and $3,500 in bonds. How many dollars of stocks should she sell (and put into bonds) to get back to 60/40?', answer: 900, tolerance: 1, unit: '$', explain: 'Target stocks: 60% x $11,000 = $6,600. She holds $7,500, so she sells $900 and buys $900 of bonds, ending at $6,600 / $4,400.' },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: 'Rebalance the tax-smart way', html: `<p>In a regular taxable account, selling winners to rebalance can trigger capital gains taxes. Common workarounds: direct <strong>new contributions</strong> into the underweight asset instead of selling, or do the rebalancing inside <strong>tax-advantaged accounts</strong> like an IRA or 401(k), where trades do not create a tax bill. Many target-date funds also rebalance automatically for you.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: 'Rebalancing a portfolio back to its target mix usually means selling some of what has gone up and buying some of what has lagged.', answer: true, explain: 'That is exactly what it does. It keeps your risk level on target and builds in a disciplined "trim high, add low" habit.' },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Putting the whole unit together', html: `<p>You have now covered a huge amount: how companies work, how to read their numbers, charts and indicators, and how to manage risk and your own brain. Here is how it all fits:</p><ul><li><strong>Your plan</strong> starts with goals and time horizons, which set your asset allocation.</li><li><strong>Your core</strong> is diversified, low-cost and mostly left alone, apart from rebalancing.</li><li><strong>Your satellites</strong> are where analysis and trading skills get tested, always with position sizing, stops and a journal.</li><li><strong>Your rules</strong> protect you from FOMO, revenge trading and overconfidence.</li></ul><p>None of this is advice about what to buy. It is a framework for making your own decisions with your eyes open. Head to My Desk, set up a paper core and a small paper satellite, and run a month of practice with a journal. That month will teach you more than any single lesson could.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'happy', say: "Survive first, learn second, grow third. Do them in that order and you will still be here in ten years." } },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: 'What is the main factor in choosing an asset allocation for a specific goal?', options: ['Which stock is trending on social media', 'The time horizon until you need the money', 'The day of the week', 'The current RSI of the market'], answer: 1, explain: 'Time horizon sets how much short-term volatility you can afford. Risk tolerance then fine-tunes it.' },
          { q: 'In a core-and-satellite portfolio, what usually makes up the core?', options: ['A few hot individual stocks', 'Day trading strategies', 'Broad, low-cost, diversified funds', 'Options and futures'], answer: 2, explain: 'The core is diversified and cheap. Satellites hold the smaller, riskier, active bets.' },
          { q: 'A 70/30 portfolio target has drifted to 78/22. With 5-percentage-point rebalancing bands, what should happen?', options: ['Nothing, bands ignore drift', 'Buy more stocks', 'Sell all stocks', 'Rebalance, because stocks are 8 points above target'], answer: 3, explain: '78% is 8 points over the 70% target, outside a 5-point band, so it is time to trim stocks and add bonds.' },
          { q: 'Why was the Enron collapse especially painful for many of its employees?', options: ['Much of their retirement savings was in the same company that employed them', 'They had too many bonds', 'They rebalanced too often', 'They used stop-loss orders'], answer: 0, explain: 'Their jobs and savings depended on one company. That is concentration risk at its worst.' },
          { q: 'Which is a tax-smart way to rebalance in a taxable account?', options: ['Sell everything every month', 'Direct new contributions into the underweight asset', 'Never invest new money', 'Only buy whatever went up most'], answer: 1, explain: 'Adding new money to the lagging asset moves you back toward target without selling winners and triggering taxes.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: 'Leo has a $20,000 account, risks 1%, and plans to buy at $40 with a structure-based stop at $37. How many shares should he buy, and what is his dollar risk?', options: ['500 shares, $1,500 risk', '200 shares, $200 risk', '66 shares, $200 risk', '50 shares, $150 risk'], answer: 2, explain: 'Dollar risk = $20,000 x 1% = $200. Risk per share = $3. $200 / $3 = 66.7, round down to 66 shares (about $198 at risk).' },
    { q: 'A strategy wins 35% of the time with an average win of 2.5R and an average loss of 1R. What is its expectancy before costs, and what does that mean?', options: ['-0.13R; it loses money', '+0.875R; it wins most trades', '+1.5R; costs do not matter', '+0.225R; it makes money on average despite losing most trades'], answer: 3, explain: '(0.35 x 2.5) - (0.65 x 1) = 0.875 - 0.65 = +0.225R per trade. A positive edge, though thin enough that costs and execution matter.' },
    { q: 'Dev is down 50% after a run of oversized trades. He says he will double up to get back to even quickly. What does the math and psychology say?', options: ['He needs a 100% gain to recover, and doubling size after losses is revenge trading that raises risk of ruin', 'He needs a 50% gain, so doubling up is reasonable', 'Losses and gains are symmetric, so it does not matter', 'He should remove all stops to give trades room'], answer: 0, explain: 'A 50% loss needs a 100% gain. Raising size after losses is classic tilt and makes another deep drawdown more likely.' },
    { q: 'A backtest tuned on 2015-2021 shows huge returns, used today\'s list of index members, and assumed every order filled at the exact price. Which problems are present?', options: ['None; this is a clean test', 'Possible overfitting, survivorship bias, and ignored slippage and costs', 'Only look-ahead bias', 'Only that the period was too long'], answer: 1, explain: 'Heavy tuning risks overfitting, current-member lists drop the failures (survivorship), and perfect fills ignore real-world costs.' },
    { q: 'Leo wants to day trade with money he needs for a graduate school tuition bill in 8 months, using a margin account with $6,000. Which concerns apply?', options: ['None, as long as she uses RSI', 'Only that commissions are high', 'Short time horizon money should not be at risk, the long-standing PDT rule could restrict frequent day trades under $25,000 (check the current rule), and most day traders lose money', 'Only that VWAP resets daily'], answer: 2, explain: 'Money needed soon belongs somewhere safe, the PDT rule (which has been under revision) may limit him, and research shows most day traders lose money.' },
  ],
};
